/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A tracking radar closes a loop: antenna, receiver, angle error detector, servo amplifier, motor and back to the antenna.
   - ε is the angle error signal (a voltage, or a ratio in monopulse); θ is the angle of the target off boresight in degrees; k is the error slope, steeper for a narrower beam.
   - The servo turns the antenna until ε is zero, and keeps doing so as the target moves.
   - Sequential lobing (lobe switching) switches between two beams squinted either side of boresight.
   - ε = (A1 − A2) / (A1 + A2) Here A1 and A2 are the echo amplitudes in the two beams; for example 1.1 and 0.9 give ε = 0.2/2.0 = 0.1.
   - Conical scan uses one beam, squinted off axis and rotated about the boresight at a few tens of revolutions per second, so the beam traces a cone.
   - Both methods compare echoes taken at different times.
   - Monopulse (simultaneous lobing) forms all the beams at once.
   - Σ is the sum channel, a normal beam on boresight, used for detection and range.
   - Δaz is the azimuth difference: left column minus right column; zero when the target is on boresight in azimuth.
*/
