/* book Worked example: Q: An echo of Pr = 1 × 10⁻¹³ W and width τ = 1 µs reaches a receiver of noise figure 10 dB at T0 = 290 K with a 1 MHz noise bandwidth. Find the noise power and input S/N, N0, the pulse energy, and the matched-filter output E/N0 and 2E/N0.
   1. F = 10 dB → 10. Noise power N = kT0FB = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 290 × 10 × 10⁶ = 4.00 × 10⁻¹⁴ W.
   2. Input S/N = 1 × 10⁻¹³ / 4.00 × 10⁻¹⁴ = 2.50 (4.0 dB).
   3. N0 = kT0F = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 290 × 10 = 4.00 × 10⁻²⁰ W/Hz.
   4. E = Prτ = 1 × 10⁻¹³ × 1 × 10⁻⁶ = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.
   5. E/N0 = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ / 4.00 × 10⁻²⁰ = 2.50 (4.0 dB); 2E/N0 = 5.00 (7.0 dB).
   Answer: N = 4.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ W, input S/N = 2.5 (4.0 dB), N0 = 4.0 × 10⁻²⁰ W/Hz, E = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. On the average-power basis the matched filter gives E/N0 = 2.5, the value the S/N in a 1/τ bandwidth also gives, since Pr/(N0B) = E/N0 when B = 1/τ; 2E/N0 = 5.0 is the same result counted on the peak-power basis, not an extra 3 dB of gain. */
