/* book Worked example 2: Q: A square-law detector must give Pfa = 10⁻⁴. (a) Find the threshold as a multiple of the mean noise power when the noise level is known. (b) Find α for a cell-averaging CFAR with N = 16 training cells. (c) How much higher is the CFAR threshold, in dB?
   1. Known noise: Pfa = exp(−T/μ), so T/μ = −ln 10⁻⁴ = 9.21.
   2. CFAR: α = N(Pfa−1/N − 1) = 16 × (104/16 − 1) = 16 × (1.7783 − 1) = 12.45.
   3. Check: (1 + 12.45/16)−16 = (1.778)−16 = 1.0 × 10⁻⁴.
   4. Ratio: 12.45/9.21 = 1.352, or 10 log10 1.352 = 1.31 dB.
   Answer: T = 9.21μ with known noise; α = 12.45 for 16 training cells, a threshold 1.31 dB higher, which is roughly the CFAR loss. With 32 cells α falls to 10.67, only 0.64 dB above. */
