/* book Worked example: Q: A radar transmitter gives 100 kW peak, and the receiver front end can safely absorb 10 mW. Find the duplexer isolation needed. A TR tube gives 60 to 70 dB: why is a receiver protector still fitted?
   1. Pt = 100 × 10³ W = 10⁵ W; Psafe = 10 × 10⁻³ W = 10⁻² W.
   2. Ratio: 10⁵ / 10⁻² = 10⁷.
   3. I = 10 log10 10⁷ = 70 dB. In dBm: +80 dBm must come down to +10 dBm.
   4. A tube giving 60 to 70 dB meets this only at its best, and the spike at the start of the pulse leaks more than the steady value, so a diode limiter after the duplexer clips what gets through.
   Answer: The duplexer must give 70 dB; because real tubes are only just good enough and the leading-edge spike is larger, a limiter protects the receiver. */
