/* book exam traps: Treating duplexer isolation as one number for all time. The leading-edge spike and the recovery period decide receiver damage and the minimum range. | Mixing up peak and average power. Average power is peak times duty cycle, and cooling follows the average. | Calling the magnetron an amplifier. It is an oscillator; the klystron and the TWT are amplifiers that keep phase coherence. | Saying a cosecant-squared beam gives up gain near the horizon. It keeps high gain near the horizon (far targets) and gives up gain at high elevation (near targets). | Thinking a radome is just a rain cover. A wet or badly designed radome lowers gain and raises sidelobes. */
